What Do International Standards Say About Contractor Safety?
ISO 45001
ISO 45001 provides an occupational health and safety management-system framework that addresses procurement, contractors, outsourced activities, operational control, worker participation and continual improvement.

It supports systematic management of risks arising from contractors and their activities.

ISO 14001
ISO 14001 addresses environmental management, including environmental aspects associated with activities that organisations control or influence.

For construction contractors, this may involve waste handling, dust, spills, noise and other environmental impacts.

ILO Construction Safety Guidance
The International Labour Organization's construction safety guidance emphasises coordination, preventive measures and responsibilities among parties involved in construction work.

National Legal Requirements
Legal duties differ by country.

For example, the United Kingdom's Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015 establish defined duties for clients, principal contractors, contractors and other dutyholders.

In the United States, OSHA requirements and the multi-employer citation policy can be relevant when several employers operate at one worksite.

Other countries have their own frameworks.

Contractual allocation of duties must always be considered alongside applicable statutory requirements.

Construction Contractor Safety Management Checklist
Use this checklist during contractor mobilisation and periodic project reviews.

Are contractor roles and responsibilities clearly documented?

Has contractor competence been evaluated for the intended work?

Are required licences, training and qualifications verified?

Have site-specific risks been communicated?

Are contractor supervisors clearly identified?

Are shared hazards and SIMOPS interfaces assessed?

Are high-risk activities subject to appropriate work controls?

Are critical controls physically verified?

Are changes communicated across affected contractors?

Are incidents and near misses reported and investigated?

Are corrective actions assigned and verified before closure?

Are emergency arrangements understood by all contractors?

Is contractor performance reviewed using meaningful indicators?

Are environmental responsibilities included where relevant?

Frequently Asked Questions
Is the main contractor responsible for subcontractor safety?
Main contractors commonly have significant responsibilities for site coordination and shared hazards. However, their precise duties depend on applicable law and their role and control. Subcontractors also retain their own legal obligations.

Can a subcontractor take full responsibility for safety through a contract?
A contract can allocate responsibilities between parties, but it does not necessarily remove statutory duties imposed on other organisations.

What is construction contractor safety management software?
It is software used to organise and track contractor safety information, onboarding requirements, work authorisations, inspections, incidents, corrective actions and performance reviews.

How can AI improve subcontractor safety management?
AI may assist with analysing incident narratives, identifying recurring deficiencies and prioritising safety reviews. It should not replace competent supervision, field verification or legal assessment.

What is SIMOPS in construction?
SIMOPS means simultaneous operations. It refers to activities occurring at the same time that may affect one another, requiring coordination to manage shared or interacting hazards.

How does ISO 45001 apply to subcontractors?
ISO 45001 includes requirements relevant to contractor procurement, coordination and control of OH&S risks associated with contractors and outsourced activities.

Conclusion: Safety Accountability Must Extend Beyond Contract Boundaries
Subcontractor safety is not simply a matter of deciding who should be blamed after an incident.

It is about establishing clear responsibilities before work begins and ensuring that critical controls remain effective as multiple contractors operate together.

Clients, main contractors, subcontractors, supervisors and workers all have roles to play, although their legal duties differ.

Connected EHS systems and responsible AI can help organisations improve coordination, information visibility and accountability.

But no digital system can substitute for competent supervision, effective controls and a culture where people act when conditions become unsafe.

The question every construction leader should ask is not only, “Who is responsible for subcontractor safety?”

It is, “Have we made every responsibility clear—and verified that the controls are actually working?”

